# The server

AppKit's Express server, its plugins, and the app's own routes. How it fits
together is in `docs/design/architecture.md`; the platform's limits are in
`docs/design/platform.md`.

## Before adding a route

- Reading from a SQL warehouse needs no route: add a `.sql` file to
  `config/queries/` and read it with AppKit's hooks through `client/src/acl/`.
  The same goes for Unity Catalog files, through the `files` plugin.
- Custom routes are for writes, Databricks APIs that have no plugin, logic over
  several steps, external services and file processing. Writes wait until the
  team chooses where they go (`docs/open-questions.md`).

## Writing a route

- Register it in `onPluginsReady` with `appkit.server.extend(app => ...)`, or in
  a custom AppKit plugin's `injectRoutes()`.
- Validate the body, the parameters and the query string with Zod before using
  any of them, and answer 400 when they don't parse.
- Wrap every `async` handler in `asyncHandler` from `server/async-handler.ts`.
  AppKit runs Express 4, although the project has Express 5's type definitions,
  and Express 4 ignores the promise an `async` handler returns: a throw inside
  it never reaches the error middleware, and the request hangs. The wrapper
  passes the error to `next(err)`. Test that your route answers with an error
  when its handler fails.
- Test plugins and routes with `@databricks/appkit/testing`, which needs no
  workspace and no network.
- Document every export with JSDoc.

## The platform

- Log to stdout and stderr only. Nothing else is kept.
- Nothing written to disk survives a restart or reaches another instance; the
  app may run as several instances behind one URL.
- On SIGTERM the app has 15 seconds before it is killed. Finish or drop work
  within that.
- No root and no system packages: everything comes from npm.
- The `database()` plugin's generated routes act as the app itself, never as the
  user, and check no one's right to a row. While it is registered, the build
  fails until a document in `docs/design/` has a "Per-user authorization"
  section saying how that is handled.
- The first route that isn't a `GET` fails the build until a document in
  `docs/design/` has a "Write target" section saying where writes go.

## Domain rules

The server never imports from `client/`. When a route needs a rule the client's
domain layer already has, the whole layer moves from `client/src/domain/` to
`shared/domain/`, and both sides import it from there. Never copy the rules.
`docs/design/architecture.md` lists the steps.
